---
title: Gingerbread cookies
servings: 36
yield: 3 to 3 1/2 dozen 3-inch cookies
tags: [vegetarian, christmas, winter]
cuisine: american
time: 1 hour 45 minutes
source: Gingerbread Cookies by PJ Hamel, King Arthur Baking, https://www.kingarthurbaking.com/recipes/gingerbread-cookies-recipe
---

Melt the @unsalted butter{170%g} in a #saucepan{} over low heat. Stir in the
@light brown sugar{159%g}(packed, or dark), @molasses{255%g},
@table salt{1%tsp}, @ground cinnamon{2%tsp}, @ground ginger{2%tsp} and
@ground allspice{1/4%tsp}(or ground cloves).

Pour it into a #medium bowl{} and let it cool to lukewarm. Beat in the
@eggs{1}.

Whisk the @baking powder{1%tsp} and @baking soda{1/2%tsp} into the
@all-purpose flour{450%g}, then stir it into the molasses mixture. The dough
will be soft and sticky.

> Don't add more flour. Chilling is what makes it rollable.

Split the dough in half, pat each into a thick rectangle, wrap, and chill at
least ~{1%hour}, until cold right through.

Heat the oven to 350°F. Have a few #baking sheets{} ready; they need no grease.

Roll the dough 1/8 to 1/4 inch thick, flouring it or rolling it between
parchment and plastic wrap if it sticks. Thinner gives a crisper cookie.

Cut shapes with a floured #cookie cutter{}, as close together as you can, and
set them on the sheets.

Bake ~{8%minutes} to 12 minutes, until the edges just brown or the cookies feel
firm. Let them sit on the sheet a few minutes, then cool on a #wire rack{}.
Repeat with the rest of the dough.

> Decorate with royal icing or a simple glaze once they are completely cool.
